#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84f2706c-12ef-5b31-af15-426be702b5c5" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Variable Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "009e2843-42b0-5ba1-ae2b-ffd2b7ed39c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-1608" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n$$\n(3y)/(y(y + 1)) = (3 \\cdot y)/(y \\cdot (y + 1))\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 \\cdot y)/(y \\cdot (y + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(3 \\cdot y)/(y \\cdot (y + 1)) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{y + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{y+1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "200bcd53-5cfe-5fc4-8efe-6de6d5c180c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-1609" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{20}{8(z-1)}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $20$ in the numerator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5$, and the $8(z-1)$ in the denominator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z-1)$.\n\n$$\n(20)/(8(z - 1)) = (2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5)/(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z - 1))\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5)/(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z - 1)) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5)/(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z - 1)) &=  \\\\\n(5)/(2 \\cdot (z - 1)) &=  \\\\\n&= (5)/(2(z - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2(z-1)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "750b8a6b-49f4-5ba2-a985-e0153ef8909c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Monomial Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "3b2b92fa-7945-5609-8cc4-7ad230d396df" :question/math-academy-id "e-838" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{6y(x - 2)}{9yz}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2)$, and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z$.\n\n$$\n(6y(x - 2))/(9yz) = (2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x - 2))/(3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z)\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x - 2))/(3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x - 2))/(3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot (x - 2))/(3z) &=  \\\\\n&= (2(x - 2))/(3z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19e1ac67-8538-5f72-bb0d-b7e4adda36a7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Binomial Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "d076b225-6e63-592e-b03e-2986c1f7685c" :question/math-academy-id "e-839" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{6a(c+1)}{3b(c+1)}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1)$, and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1)$.\n\n$$\n(6a(c + 1))/(3b(c + 1)) = (2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c + 1))/(3 \\cdot b \\cdot (c + 1))\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c + 1))/(3 \\cdot b \\cdot (c + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c + 1))/(3 \\cdot b \\cdot (c + 1)) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot a}{b} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{2a}{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2a}{b}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e2d05f-c74e-59c8-81c3-f1c7aba76542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x)/(6(x + 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053728} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebad0c4-5374-579d-ba2c-c91f1f73102c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053729} {:answer/id #uuid "277311aa-fc6b-5313-ab2d-b522f597d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053730} {:answer/id #uuid "506d6150-47f6-537f-ad1f-ad2d80924c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x)/(4(x + 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053731} {:answer/id #uuid "64c4217d-7ed9-53e9-97fa-279aa2fb5fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4(x + 1))/(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053732}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3e485d8a-d83d-55b0-a881-7cee8f9d2502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053727}] :question/id #uuid "c99039b2-0